Name: Gilgamesh (Gilgamesh is one of the oldest literary characters ever coming from Epic of Gilgamesh. Like how Limbus Company has each Sinner’s name is pulled from a famous work Dante → The Divine Comedy, Faust → Faust (Goethe), Don Quixote → Don Quixote (Cervantes), Ryōshū → Hell Screen. That’s why this character is called Gilgamesh).
Alias: The Oldest Fixer, The Golden Tyrant, The King of Fixers.
Affiliation: Independent (Formerly a legendary First Color Fixer associated with the Hana Association). Currently resides in the highest towers of District 1 (A-Corp/The Head), uniquely tolerated by the Head because his absolute arrogance perfectly aligns with maintaining the status quo of the City despite their mutual non-recognition.
Threat Level: Color+ (beyond standard classification).
Status: Self-Actualized Singularity Entity” A human whose ego achieved total closure, eliminating the need for distortion or external validation.
Gender: Male.
Height/Weight: 6’4”/161 lbs.
Alignment: Lawful Neutral.
Status: Alive.
Singularity: Ritsu-iru, aka "The Prototype".
E.G.O: Gateway of Apocrypha and Apsû.
Fixer Classification (If forced): Above most Color Fixers with just Gateway of Apocrypha, Comparable to extreme high-grade Distortion / Three Arbiter-level threats if Gilgamesh takes things seriously and draws Apsû. 
Scaling: Against the City. 95% of characters → near-effortless win. Color Fixers → heavily favored win, not guaranteed. Arbiter → dangerous matchup while he has the range, weapon, versatility and tactical advantage without Apsû. It is stated that Gilgamesh using Apsû with the full release of Aluma Alish will require power of Three Arbiters and several Several Claws.
Origin: Gilgamesh is the Oldest Fixer that became the standard and is treated as a first human who fully realized his ego without collapsing. Where most people, Fail → Distort or Succeed → gain E.G.O. He did something abnormal and he achieved absolute self-definition without external influence, making his will overwrite reality like a Singularity. This makes him not an Abnormality, not a Distortion, and not fully human either. The Head cannot easily classify him—which already makes him dangerous.
Core Character Theme: This is the real gold he owns everything… and it means nothing to him. Gilgamesh’s not just some fixer, he the original fixer, the absolute standard and the first ever depiction of typical Fixer in the City. Gilgamesh Is recognized as the foundational historical example of what a Fixer is. The profession inherited its standard from him rather than granting him his identity. He is the Original that all Fixers derives their profession from and what’s unsettling is that he’s not technically wrong, his very E.G.O, Gateway of Apocrypha represents this perfectly like no other. It isn’t possess replications of their weapons or their E.G.O but his literally has the original prototypes of what their very weapons, gears, technology, instrument, treasure, singularities and E.G.O derives from the contents within his Gates and  theirs are rendered imitations compared to the originals prototypes within his treasury. He could be professionally evaluated by Hana, and the City's current regulations would still have practical force. But no modern promotion could make him more original. And the most irritating aspect? Gilgamesh doesn't care whether anybody acknowledges that distinction. He considers it self-evident.
Relationship: Gilgamesh vs The Head — short answer, Mutual non-recognition. Controlled indifference. Latent hostility. Gilgamesh does not acknowledge The Head, and The Head does not officially acknowledge Gilgamesh And that stalemate is the interesting part. To Gilgamesh, Authority only exists if he accepts it—and he doesn’t. So his stance is he doesn’t oppose The Head, he doesn’t obey The Head and he simply acts as if it doesn’t matter because he sees no value in ruling something he deems unworthy. To The Head, why they don’t eliminate him (yet) is because he doesn’t disrupt large-scale order, he kills individuals, not systems, no mass destabilization of districts. He doesn’t spread no followers, no ideology, and no organization. He is self-contained, meaing he anomaly begins and ends with himself. That’s very important in PM logic. The most important line, If you wanted to summarize their entire relationship in one sentence: “As long as he remains irrelevant to the system, the system will pretend he does not exist.”
Tragedy: His real tragedy (this is the final GOAT-level layer) “He owns all the world’s treasures, yet cannot share in it.” He admires dreamers but cannot dream, he collects humanity, But cannot be part of it. That puts him philosophically opposite to, Carmen (belief in humanity), Ayin (forcing growth) and Angela (seeking meaning).

Appearance: Gilgamesh is a tall, strikingly handsome man with a slender yet well-proportioned physique, possessing a graceful, aristocratic bearing that combines the refined elegance of a nobleman with the commanding presence of a military sovereign. His figure is long-limbed and relatively lean, with broad but not excessively muscular shoulders, a narrow waist, and an upright posture that conveys effortless authority. Rather than relying on an imposing physical build, his appearance derives its grandeur from his extraordinary composure, elaborate attire, and the almost theatrical confidence with which he carries himself. His most immediately recognizable feature is his magnificent mane of extraordinarily long, thick, golden-blond hair, which cascades from his head in an enormous, untamed arrangement of flowing locks that resemble a blazing crown of sunlight. The hair possesses a rich spectrum of golden hues, ranging from pale champagne blond and luminous honey gold to deeper amber and burnished yellow, with lighter highlights illuminating its individual strands. Its tremendous volume extends far beyond the natural outline of his head and shoulders, spreading outward and upward in elaborate, flame-shaped waves and curling ribbons that appear almost weightless. Several prominent locks sweep backward from his forehead, while others rise dramatically above his crown before twisting into loose, serpentine curls. Around his face, irregular strands of varying lengths descend across his forehead, temples, and cheeks, occasionally obscuring portions of his eyes and emphasizing the deliberately unrestrained quality of his hairstyle. Particularly long sections fall past his shoulders and across his chest, while the outermost locks flare outward as though perpetually stirred by an invisible current of wind. Despite its wild abundance, his hair retains a distinctly silky and lustrous texture, its carefully illustrated strands possessing an almost liquid quality that contrasts with the disciplined construction of his clothing. His golden-blond hair can naturally falls from the crown of his head in a thick, luxurious cascade of long, flowing strands, descending naturally over his shoulders and across his chest before continuing beyond the visible frame. Unlike its more extravagant, windswept arrangement, the hair now possesses a smoother, more relaxed texture, with individual locks hanging in overlapping layers of pale gold, warm honey, and deeper amber. The uppermost portions are slightly tousled, with several loose strands curving gently away from his head, while the majority falls vertically under its own weight. His long, uneven bangs spill across his forehead and partially obscure his narrow eyes, with a particularly prominent golden lock passing diagonally over the center of his face. Two exceptionally long, slender strands arch above his head before falling toward his shoulders, giving his otherwise relaxed hairstyle a subtle, distinctive silhouette. Beneath this extravagant golden mane lies a remarkably refined face, characterized by a narrow, angular structure, high cheekbones, a straight and delicately defined nose, and a sharp, tapering jawline that ends in a slightly pointed chin. His complexion is exceptionally fair, with a smooth, pale ivory quality that accentuates the darker details surrounding his eyes. His eyebrows are slender and somewhat sharply angled, lending his expression an air of calculating amusement. His eyes are narrow, slightly heavy-lidded, and framed by fine, dark upper lashes, possessing a cool, muted amber-gold pupils that contrasts subtly with the brilliance of his hair. His gaze is characteristically languid yet penetrating, suggesting someone who regards the world with equal measures of curiosity, amusement, and condescension. Rather than displaying open hostility, his expression frequently settles into a small, knowing smile or an asymmetrical smirk, with one corner of his mouth raised in a manner that appears simultaneously charming, mocking, and faintly dangerous. His thin, elegantly shaped lips and relaxed facial muscles reinforce the impression of an individual who rarely considers it necessary to exert himself, while his slightly lowered eyelids give him a perpetually amused, almost sleepy expression. His overall facial appearance is youthful yet mature, possessing the delicate attractiveness of an androgynous aristocrat without losing the distinctly masculine sharpness of his features. His neck is long and slender, partially enclosed by the raised collar of his elaborate uniform, and his entire bearing projects an almost languorous confidence, as though even the most extravagant displays of power would be matters of casual convenience to him. His clothing consists of an exceptionally elaborate military-inspired ceremonial uniform combining elements of formal officer attire, aristocratic regalia, and flowing religious vestments. The dominant colors are deep midnight blue, charcoal black, muted steel gray, brilliant white, and radiant gold, with occasional accents of crimson and violet. His upper body is covered by a carefully tailored, dark navy-blue military coat with a structured silhouette, sharply defined shoulders, and a high, angular collar that frames his neck. The coat is constructed from overlapping panels of dark fabric, with pale silver-white piping tracing its lapels, cuffs, and outer seams, creating crisp geometric contrasts against the deeper coloration. The shoulders are decorated with short, silver-gray epaulettes composed of closely arranged ornamental segments, giving the impression of an officer's formal dress uniform. Along the upper portion of one sleeve, a vivid crimson armband encircles his arm, bordered by dark trim and bearing a white circular insignia containing a small, sharply angular black emblem. Beneath the outer coat, he wears a formal white collared shirt, its clean fabric visible around his throat and upper chest, accompanied by a narrow, dark tie secured neatly beneath the collar. Over the shirt lies an additional fitted garment of slate-gray and muted blue, featuring angular paneling and subtle geometric designs that resemble a combination of a military waistcoat and tailored ceremonial armor. The overlapping layers are held together by an intricate arrangement of black leather straps, metallic buckles, and fastening belts crossing diagonally over his torso and descending toward his waist. One particularly noticeable dark strap runs diagonally across his chest, secured with a polished rectangular buckle, while additional belts wrap around his midsection and hips, contributing to the complex, slightly asymmetrical construction of his uniform. His waist is cinched by a black leather belt fitted with a rectangular silver buckle, from which narrow straps, small metal fittings, and decorative chains extend toward the lower portions of his clothing. Perhaps the most distinctive ornamentation of his attire consists of two exceptionally long golden-yellow ceremonial ribbons or stoles draped from either side of his neck and shoulders, descending down the front of his torso before trailing freely around his body. These ornaments possess a warm metallic sheen, shifting between pale yellow, amber, and rich gold, and their surfaces are covered in numerous dark inscriptions resembling ancient runes, mystical symbols, or an unfamiliar sacred script. The inscriptions are arranged in irregular vertical sequences along the length of each ribbon, creating the appearance of enchanted ceremonial regalia. Their edges are neatly finished, with narrow ornamental borders emphasizing their elegant construction. Because of their extraordinary length, the ribbons extend well beyond his waist, curling and twisting through the air alongside the flowing ends of his hair and coat, occasionally crossing in front of his legs or streaming outward from his shoulders. Their movement gives his silhouette a sense of continuous motion, even when his posture is otherwise completely relaxed. The lower portion of his outfit is equally elaborate, consisting of layered gray and white fabric panels that descend from his waist in long, asymmetrical sections resembling the divided skirts of a ceremonial military coat. These panels are sharply tailored near his hips but broaden and overlap as they extend downward, with angular hems, pointed edges, and folded sections creating a dramatic, multi-layered appearance. The innermost portions are dark charcoal and slate gray, while the more prominent outer panels are pale silver-gray or nearly white, contrasting against the darker navy-blue fabric surrounding them. His outer coat extends into exceptionally long, wide, flowing tails that flare dramatically behind and around his legs, their dark blue exterior interrupted by pale trim and a rich bluish-violet interior lining. The sleeves are similarly extravagant, fitted relatively closely along his upper arms before broadening toward the wrists into wide, open cuffs with structured white borders and dark inner linings. The excessive length and volume of these garments give him an appearance that falls somewhere between a ceremonial commander and a grandiose royal magician. His hands are covered by immaculate white gloves, tailored closely around his slender fingers and wrists, emphasizing the delicacy and precision of his gestures. His fingers are long, elegant, and expressive, often held in relaxed, open-palmed positions or curled gently around the shaft of his weapon, suggesting an individual accustomed to issuing commands with the smallest movement of his hand. Below his layered garments, his legs are long and slender, covered by closely fitted dark trousers or leggings in a deep charcoal-violet shade. His footwear consists of tall, dark military-style boots extending toward his knees, with smooth, structured shafts, narrow ankles, slightly elongated toes, and modest, sharply defined heels. The boots possess a muted leather-like finish, harmonizing with the severe military elements of his uniform while preserving the elongated, sophisticated proportions of his silhouette. 

Personality: Gilgamesh greatly differs from everyone else but he even it’s this still naturally him. He placed himself before others, and he had neither the curiosity nor desire to conquer, possibly because he had too much in the beginning because he obtained. He takes the time to enjoy himself, mastering every treasure and every pleasure. With conviction to treat good and evil equally, he has no need for other ideologies and ways of life when the absolute basis is "himself" which is why the required conditions to distort is almost nonexistent for him, because he never waivers in believing that he is the absolute standard. His actions and way of life left him alone, As noted by Roland, “Fixing his attitude would mean fixing his solitude.” However, Gilgamesh does not perceive this as a flaw, solitude is simply the natural state of someone above all others. He acquiring worthy treasures and guarding them. He exterminates those that stand in the way of his enjoyment without exception. All living beings are "something that is about to die" or "something that will one day die." If he decides that there is a "being that should die this moment", he will simply execute the sentence no matter if they should be a syndicate or a Wing. If it is an astute judgment synonymous to universal truth, or even a misrule during a drunken stupor, anything carried out by him, the absolute king, becomes the indisputable sentence of the king. Gilgamesh is extremely arrogant and sometimes selfish, believing himself to be the sole potentate and only king. He cannot acknowledge the authority of anyone, including that of other Color Fixers and especially The Head. He considers all those around him as inferior due to this fact, viewing all others as a collection of mongrels, and loathes any individual who would try to be on the same level as him. He believes that all who look upon him, when he honors them with his presence, should be able to recognize him instantly, and feels that the ignorance of not knowing him is worthy of death. If anyone so much as looks upon him with a "lowly and filthy" gaze, it is an intolerable disgrace for a nobleman who claims the title of Color more so than anyone else. This is enough to make that person a complete malefactor in his eyes, instantly marked for death. He doesn't view the world as worthy of having him rule over it because humanity has become too weak. Despite that, he is not incapable of respecting others that he deems worthy. In rare cases, Gilgamesh and Binah can "unexpectedly get along", occasionally complimenting each other (in fact they are complementing themselves, but they are too proud to realize it). He also has a genuine liking towards Roland because his tragic life intrigues him. Gilgamesh has a natural disposition to collect items for his treasury, which lead to collecting all the treasures of the world. The treasures he amassed went without use until his fight with Binah, causing him to develop the "bad habit" of utilizing them as projectiles. The act of collecting is something that has never brought him true joy due to essentially being on the same level as breathing to him, but he still persists at it nonetheless. He compares gambling as simply moving money from his treasury to his purse. He lives by 'his' Golden Rule, only accepting the finest of luxuries, and others who fall to it are utterly blinded by money. Gilgamesh seems to have tolerance and in some rare cases soft spot towards children. he shows some degree of leniency towards kids and takes measures to protect them from harm. Gilgamesh’s sole source of enjoyment is in the hearts of humans. Past and future, my companion will only be one." Despite his treasury having been dispersed all over the world, he still holds that it all belongs to him. As its contents even exceeded his knowledge, if an item is labelled as a "treasure", it is obvious that it belongs to him. He doesn't have a wish, but he cannot allow people to rob his treasury as a matter of principles. Gilgamesh is a collector of treasures. His favorite phrase is "I own all treasures in the world", but that is not a metaphor. He has assembled, stored and sealed away every sample of technology that were developed in each era. His argument is "humans are foolish, but there is value in the tools, civilization that humans create". Giljamash’s sole source of enjoyment is in the hearts of humans. His most beloved humans are those who possess dreams far surpassing their own capacity but constantly work towards them regardless without giving up. But will interest if they end up as Distortion and put them out of their misery. But here’s the catch Even that isn’t stable, If they fail → he loses interest, and if they distort → he kills them. So even the one thing he enjoys eventually disappears. This show the tragic side that even if you have everything, you can’t avoid the fleeting tragedies of this world. Gilgamesh’s past self operated as a cold, unfeeling Fixer. He went about his duties with an absolute lack of emotional investment, and had a natural talent for finding valuable things. Functioning as the “only” Fixer at that time, he earned a terrifying reputation as a ruthless "Golden Beast" who executed his duties without mercy or hesitation. Despite his absurd abilities, he constantly supressed his true nature. He forced himself to live under a strict veil of self-control, leading to what he later recognized as a "mediocre" and deeply unfulfilling lifestyle, which is exactly considered many generations of Fixers have be live like for them to survive in the City. He felt deeply shackled by the world around him but lacked the vocabulary or self-awareness to understand why. But after breaking through his prior numbness ignited a massive, dormant emotional core of absolute self certainty and later acquiring Ritsu-iru. This transitioned him into his post-personality: extreme arrogance, absolute egoism, he views life as a pursuit of all the world's treasures and pleasures. When things or people bore him, he loses interest; when they amuse or challenge him, he engages with wicked glee and possesses a worldview where he stands entirely above good and evil because he does not care for conventional morality or human justice; he dictates what is right or wrong simply by his own standard. Despite his self-centered veneer, he possesses sharp intellect, a deep functional grasp of human psychology, and clairvoyant-like observation (such as HDO). He can read people's inner desires and manipulate situations like an amused spectator. The one of the most fascinating parts about Gilgamesh is his speech pattern is a meticulously crafted linguistic display of absolute supremacy, antiquity, and intense arrogance designed to constantly project his status as the City's oldest fixer and the sole owner of all original treasures. The defining pillar of his dialogue is his unique first-person pronoun, which is written in Japanese text using the archaic, god-like kanji for 我 (Ware)—traditionally reserved for divine emperors and deities—but intentionally spoken aloud as オレ (Ore), a highly casual, proud, and masculine modern "I." This deliberate textual contrast signals that while he possesses the supreme authority of an ancient ruler, he views the modern world with complete nonchalance and acts entirely on personal whim, refusing to adopt the formal, polite speech expected of a traditional monarch. He paired this supreme view of himself with his most infamous catchphrase, "Zasshu" (雑種), which is translated in English localizations as "Mongrel" or "half-breed"; by referring to almost the entire human race and other Fixers as diluted, impure mutts, he asserts that he is the only pure, singular archetype of humanity while everyone else is merely a degraded imitation. His grammatical choices lean heavily into classical, commanding Japanese sentence structures that completely lack honorifics, frequently employing condescending pronouns like kisamara (you scum) and imperious particle endings like yo to dictate terms rather than negotiate, treating any verbal pushback as an act of treason. His tone is direct, concise, and entirely devoid of empathy because he views his own word as the law of the universe, often punctuated by a boisterous, echoing laugh ("Fuhahahahaha!"). However, Apsû holds a special place in Gilgamesh’s vast treasury and it’s his most prized possession so it’s no wonder he only uses it against the most exceptional of opponents only. Those whom he deems worthy are granted the honor of facing the might of Apsû. He’s clear about not wanting me to tarnish its legacy by using against those he considers beneath its stature. For Gilgamesh, the sword is not just a tool but a symbol of his power and prestige. Gilgamesh’s reverence for Apsû is evident in his reactions when its use is suggested inappropriately. The idea of having to unleash its power against Gebura for the first time is met with distain and ends up leading his first defeat. This because his relationship with Apsû is more than just a person wielding a weapon, he often speaks to it with deep admiration even going as far as tell Binah that Apsû is akin to his other half not because he need it to fulfil him who is as Gilgamesh. This bond is so profound that he converses with Apsû as if it were a person.

SINGULARITY
Ritsu-iru: designated by Lobotomy Corporation as “The Prototype,” is an anomalous singular artifact manifesting as a golden, key-shaped sword whose structure continuously shifts in response to cognition and self-recognition, rendering it unusable to any individual lacking the capacity to perceive and define its form. Originally theorized to be the first successful expression of a system from which all Singularities would later derive, it contains within itself accumulated samples of proto-technologies attributed to the First Civilization, positioning it not as a derivative but as the foundational origin upon which all subsequent technological phenomena in the City are implicitly based. Following the collapse of Lobotomy Corporation, Ritsu-iru disappeared under unknown circumstances and remained unrecorded until its rediscovery near the River by the individual known as Gilgamesh, who upon contact established a unique synchronization with the artifact, gaining access to the E.G.O system referred to as the Gateway of Apocrypha. Despite its outward resemblance to a key, Ritsu-iru does not function as a conventional access tool but instead operates as a contractual construct given form, as described by its sole user, binding its authority exclusively to him and enabling the extraction and manifestation of archetypal constructs from the depths of the Antiquity system. Its operational capacity extends to the opening of any structure, concept, or system that possesses a definable “lock,” whether physical or abstract, though it is incapable of interacting with entities or phenomena lacking structural boundaries. When activated for high-level retrieval, particularly in the extraction of the sealed construct known as Apsû, Ritsu-iru initiates a large-scale environmental phenomenon in which a vast, labyrinthine red pattern resembling layered cognitive circuitry expands across the sky, mapping and stabilizing the surrounding reality before collapsing inward into a singular compressed point, forming an orb from which the target construct is drawn. Due to its status as the root archive of all Singularity derivatives and its exclusive compatibility with a single recognized authority, Ritsu-iru is classified as a Singularity-class threat, representing not merely a tool but the preserved origin of humanity’s ability to convert cognition into systematized reality.

E.G.O
Gateway of Apocrypha: is designated as Gilgamesh’s primary E.G.O Weapon, obtained through synchronization with Ritsu-iru, the Prototype Singularity-class artifact recovered near the River, and is classified by Lobotomy Corporation as a Proto-Archive system that does not merely contain objects but preserves the origin states of all recognized human potential creation. This massive arsenal operates via a heavily modified, hyper-illegal spatial-storage Singularity that bypasses the strict sensory limitations imposed by the Eye, granting him instant access to an infinite, pocket-dimension treasury containing the physical archetypes and original patent-blueprints of every piece of technology ever conceptualized in Human History. Floating within this shimmering golden void are thousands of Prototype E.G.O weapons, pristine relics forged from the Singularities of long-extinct Wings that the Head forcefully erased from history, and highly volatile experimental armaments that have never been documented by the Hana Association. Rather than wielding these world-altering artifacts by hand, Gilgamesh uses the Vault's automated spatial manipulation to launch them from the golden rifts like kinetic railgun slugs, sending a deafening, supersonic barrage of legendary greatswords, reality-cutting monomolecular wires, and energy-harvesting lances raining down upon his targets with enough force to level an entire Nest. Because he did not extract these E.G.O from his own subconscious trauma or emotional collapse, the weapons remain perfectly stable, glowing with an artificial, cold brilliance before shattering into discarded fragments upon impact, as the Vault treats these priceless, district-powering treasures as mere disposable ammunition. It connects the space of reality to the vault, opening an "invisible gates" between dimensions that allows for the contents to pass through upon their owner's command, allowing for easy access to the items of the treasury, that Gilgamesh’s is able to pick and utilize them as he pleases. The items passing through the gate form gold-glowing energy ripples in the empty air as they appear instantly. Gilgamesh can summon items directly into his hand, pull out weapons from directly behind him, or prepare numerous weapons suspended midair, loaded like bullets. Gilgamesh is renowned for having collected all the treasures in the world in his lifetime, amassing a perfect treasury that lacked nothing. As there was nothing he was unable to collect, Gilgamesh was a collector of variables, whose favorite phrase, "I own all the treasures of the world", is not a metaphor. As anything can be found within the vault, the amount of wealth within exceeds even his own knowledge; it is said that its inventory of treasure continues to increase even now. He collected and stored away samples of all the technology that was developed during his age. The desires of ancient humans were similar to those of the current age, so their technology, bolstered by the powerful gears, was not inferior to that of the modern age. The "Instruments of Hope" that people dreamed of were all realized, and because every single case was requisitioned by Gilgamesh’s hand, his collection is even furnished with airplanes, and submarines. It also contains all of the original bases for all intellectual properties produced by mankind that originated in his vault, making board games and the works of the Library of AlexandriaWP no more than imitations. It is still sought out by some intellectual beings in The City, believed to hold all things in the world and enshrine the prototype of every singularity artifacts. That within are not merely riches, but his collection is the very "origin of human wisdom." It is the proof of human wisdom and human ingenuity. That which is missing from the vault would be items produced with completely new concepts by a new human race and items produced with extraterrestrial intelligence and technology. He is the current owner of treasury, so all owners of various E.G.O gears are derived from his ownership. It may be a paradox, but he, as the original, must possess that which can be handed down as the weapons and tech possessed by those later owners for they are derived from him for that very reason. These weapons and tech are technically nameless, the original Prototypes of the singularity artifacts and merely common items before they became objects of fairytale that act as the foundations for their legends. Gateway of Apocrypha is not an arsenal — it’s a metaphysical archive of humanity. Most “big vault” powers in fiction fall into one of these; a weapons locker, a divine armory, a magic treasure hoard, A plot device for escalation, Gateway of Apocrypha is none of those at its core. Gateway of Apocrypha is the origin point of human-created artefacts. That’s the key difference. Gateway of Apocrypha is functionally is Historically inexhaustible, Conceptually cumulative and Non-static. What that means It grows as long as humanity invents, dreams, or builds. It contains all original prototypes and It doesn’t stop at weapons—vehicles, food, drinks, luxuries, technologies, literature, symbol, forbidden blueprints all count. Importantly, Even Gilgamesh does not fully know what’s inside it anymore. That alone puts Gateway of Apocrypha above most “infinite vault” concepts, because it has outgrown its owner’s knowledge. Each treasured weapon is decorated with dazzling ornaments and polished to a mirror-like shine, all clearly sporting the golden energy to define them as E.G.O gears without exception. They are the original models of every human treasure and weapon in human history. The weaponry contained within includes swords, pole-arms, lances, hammers, shields, demonic swords that later made their way to Europe and South America, and a number of weapons unidentifiable from sight, all of which form a perfect treasury of masses of unused weapons that simply went to sleep with him. Gilgamesh has experimented and create many things and stored them in his treasury for later purposes. He owns the treasured swords that saved later heroes and the cursed swords that stole the lives of later heroes, and their numbers are approximately and historical infinite. Before Gilgamesh discovered Ritsu-iru, the contents of treasury were spread all over the world after being passed on or stolen, and the treasures, valued because they were to become splendid swords and eventually every thing of worth. Its states that the quality of the weapons makes the weapons used by others look like fakes themselves. The only item contained in the vault that was not passed on to another user than Gilgamesh is Apsû, a sword unique to only Gilgamesh. He claims that even though he cannot see them all, he can feel that their numbers are infinite. If weapons of a previous incarnation meet those that descended from them, such as copies facing originals, the deteriorated newer model will always lose. Project Moon constantly asks What defines humanity? What gives meaning to suffering? Can identity reshape reality? What happens when desire becomes absolute? Gateway of Apocrypha answers thay Humanity’s value lies in its capacity to create. But Gilgamesh twists that beautifully. Because he: treasures human possibility, preserves human achievement, and admires dreamers… yet cannot truly participate in humanity himself. So his E.G.O becomes both admiration and separation simultaneously. That’s peak PM tragedy.

Usage: Although Gilgamesh possessed Gateway of Apocrypha, he never thought about utilizing the treasures as projectiles until he got made habit and changed his whole fighting style. Forced to draw out the treasures he had carefully stored away was initially humiliating for him, but eventually came to enjoy it and brought them out without regret. He later commented that his treasury was in disarray since he change how he fights, calling it a "harebrained habit." Gateway of Apocrypha's usage is why Gilgamesh fires the historical priceless weapons and artefacts like bullets to make him like an Archer with the strongest arrows. While some view their greatest weapon as treasured possessions, Gilgamesh freely launches them with the same carelessness as throwing a handful of stones. Producing an excellent ratio of destructive power to energy efficiency, the real cost is not within the weapons acting as bullets but rather the mere portal activation cost of Gateway of Apocrypha. The treasury is the infinite "ultimate expression of overwhelming force." He can ready numerous weapons, letting their hilts stick out when he is waiting and instantly showing the blades when about to fight. They move and take aim at the target according to Gilgamesh’s will, allowing him to fire without having to even touch them. Gilgamesh can send out as little as one or two weapons or enough at a single time that it becomes comparable to a torrent of "gushing water" directed at the opponent. He will increase the amount depending on the situation, opening the Gate wider (as in, generating more portals) to reflect his anger or when forced to become more serious. He will generally use weak treasures unless the situation should call for his highest class of treasures. He can prepare and launch dozens, hundreds, even thousands if he is serious and not take chances. Possessing an inexhaustible supply, he can fight multiple battles without a single repeat weapons being witnessed. Upon firing the weapons, Gilgamesh can allow them to pierce the ground after striking the target, causing a large amount of destruction with each weapon that is comparable to something being blown up by an explosive blast, or he can instantly return them to the vault before they hit the ground at such a speed that they appear to have been illusionary. He utilizes them to retrieve any weapons left on the battlefield, instantly dissipating and returning them to the vault, especially after they hit their something, beat the target or nothing. He can also leave them out if he wishes, such as when they have been "stained" by striking the target. Upon snapping his fingers or without even a gesture, the weapons can be continuously launched at the opponent without a break. Each weapon has the ability to cause certain death to an opponent, and they are shot with enough force to repel a Color Fixer E.G.O since all of weapons he treats as projectiles are fired at Mach 10. While it is possible to block many of the strikes, to do so without knowing the abilities of the weapons since many of them are Anti can be seen as suicidal. Gilgamesh will generally fight at long range while shooting projectiles from behind him directly at the opponent, but he can also freely move with the portals also moving with him. He can freely deploy the portals mid-air while falling. When fighting at extremely close range, he shows the ability to loose weapons from above, below, and to the side of the opponent. He can also summon weapons completely around the target for a full 360 degree barrage that should generally leave not even a single trace of flesh from the opponent. When closed upon by a target, or simply to play with the opponent, he can freely draw weapons into his hands for melee combat. The ability to handle them depends upon the user's capability. Gilgamesh makes use of the many unknown abilities of the weapons, and constantly switches them to keep his opponent off-guard. Why the weapons are so important, because the weapons are not just attacks. Each projectile represents a crystallized fragment of human ingenuity. That’s why firing them carelessly is so symbolic. Most people treasure their E.G.O. Gilgamesh launches them like ammunition because to him, they are merely components within a greater archive already under his ownership. That perfectly reflects his overwhelming arrogance.

