LIMITATION
Absolute Ego: The reasons Gilgamesh sometimes doesn’t win every single fight that he should absolutely win with relative ease as he definitely should, it’s because his standard are way too high. He is Gilgamesh, he is the owner of Ritsu-iru, a man who possesses all the treasuries of the world and the first and arguably the strongest fixer in the City. He has Gateway of Apocrypha, a dimensional vault that works like a treasury that holds inside every single original of every weapon and artifact created by mankind, including the ones that killed every single Fixer, Syndicate, and Arbiters in history. So essentially, he has the weaknesses to every single one of his opponents. He has Apsû, Sword of the Truthful Rupture, the pinnacle of his treasury and it said to arguably the pinnacle of all E.G.O in The City. His ego so strong, he can refuse most types of mental interference and never waivers, which is why he can never distort, because he has no need changing who is if he sees himself as the standard. His Higher-Dimensional Overseer gives him such high level form of clairvoyance that he can actually perceive nearly all outcomes, read an opponent’s abilities by just looking at them and know the most optimal path to victory. So with how overpowered he is, what exactly is his main weakness? The irony on that he is his own weakness. Gilgamesh more than likely capable of winning of winning and all that brawl with all Color Fixers by himself. The only thing stops him is his own arrogance, he plays too much with this food and refuses to fight all out even though he could easily win the battles Roland has faced if he did. The main reason why he never uses his ultimate weapon which is Apsû because he doesn’t deem anyone worthy of it just also the main reason why he once lost to The Red Mist, since She perfectly dominates him in close-range, which he could’ve easily just pulled out Apsû and completely overwhelming her in that moment but he refused to do it because of his own ego until it was way too late and ended up losing the battle. He pretty much holds back against everyone, which heavily increases the gap between what he could do and what he actually does.

Tactical Vulnerability: Because Gateway of Apocrypha often involves firing weapons from a distance, He may be exposed while concentrating. Disruption, surprise attacks, or being forced to defend themselves while managing the treasury can limit effectiveness.

The Original vs Derivative: it’s never stated that a fake can’t beat the original.

Close-Combat Transition Speed: He’s decent in melee—but: His strongest form is still ranged control, Ultra-fast blitz fighters can potentially force split-second decision.

Pride of the Sovereign: is the most defining limitation imposed upon Gilgamesh, as his absolute confidence in his treasury often leads him to dismiss opponents he deems unworthy of serious attention, deliberately restricting the output of the depth of his treasury in the early stages of battle. This is not born of ignorance but of ideology, as Gilgamesh believes that overwhelming lesser foes with his full might would cheapen both his status and the meaning of kingship, and as a result he may allow adversaries moments of survival or growth that could have been prevented through immediate annihilation. He doesn’t lose because he’s weak, he loses because he refuses to acknowledge the need to win properly.

Selective Seriousness: further compounds this flaw, as Gilgamesh rarely escalates a confrontation unless an enemy proves capable of challenging him, causing him to delay the use of his most dangerous abilities, including deeper layers of the Gateway of Apocrypha or the full release of Apsû, until his interest is genuinely piqued, a restraint that can be exploited by opponents who rely on sudden power surges, teamwork, or sacrificial strategies. This ensures He doesn’t instantly win every fight, Opponents get narrative space, His defeats (if any) feel earned.

Isolation of the King: serves as an existential limitation rather than a tactical one, as Gilgamesh’s refusal to submit, cooperate as an equal, or rely on others leaves him without allies capable of matching his level, making prolonged multi-front conflicts more taxing despite his immense power, and rendering him vulnerable to coordinated strategies designed to divide attention rather than overpower him directly. These limitations do not render Gilgamesh weak, but rather define the narrow margins by which the impossible might be achieved, for in a world governed by hierarchy and authority, even a king who claims ownership over all things is not entirely beyond consequence.
